{"chunk_id": "74cb11eee493", "text": "This section continues the famous dialogue between Yudhishthira and the mysterious Yaksha. Yudhishthira explains that true Brahmanahood depends not on learning but on righteous conduct and disciplined behavior. In a series of philosophical questions, he answers what brings happiness, the nature of wonder, and the proper path of life. He observes that humans daily see death yet still believe themselves immortal, and that the true path is the one walked by the great. After correctly answering, he is allowed to revive one brother; he chooses Nakula rather than the stronger Bhima or Arjuna, reasoning that justice requires fairness to both of his mothers. Impressed by his commitment to dharma, the Yaksha reveals himself as Yama, Yudhishthira\u2019s divine father, revives all the brothers, and grants boons, including the Pandavas\u2019 wish to remain unrecognized during their final year of exile.\nYudhishthira answers the Yaksha\u2019s riddles on virtue and happiness, chooses Nakula to revive for fairness, and the Yaksha reveals himself as Dharma, granting boons.\nThemes: dharma and virtue, moral wisdom, impartial justice, test of character, divine revelation", "vector": [0.02421075850725174, -0.02282576449215412, -0.0021024607121944427, -0.023984365165233612, -0.00930209830403328, 0.013490373268723488, -0.022665956988930702, -0.01927005872130394, -0.018138092011213303, -0.011133220046758652, 0.0022938961628824472, -0.006532110273838043, 0.02313206158578396, 0.00827001128345728, -0.01596738025546074, -0.004774233791977167, 0.05073871836066246, -0.014928635209798813, 0.04051107168197632, -0.009561784565448761, -0.0020708320662379265, -0.016566656529903412, -0.0021307598799467087, 0.005663159769028425, -0.008030300959944725, 0.01777852699160576, 0.02651464194059372, -0.01523493230342865, -0.00025906210066750646, -0.012378382496535778, -0.0011286367662250996, 0.0005031423061154783, -0.011459492146968842, 0.005223690532147884, -0.03393235